Under the Access to Information Act, any person may request access to records under the control of a government institution, regardless of their nationality or place of residence. You do not need to be a Canadian citizen or resident to make a request under the Access to Information Act. However, you may not use the Access to Information Act to obtain your personal information, nor may you use it to request access to someone else’s personal information. To obtain your personal information, you must use the Privacy Act. To request access to someone else’s personal information, you must obtain their written consent authorizing you to do so. What is the difference between the Access to Information Act and the Privacy Act? The Access to Information Act gives you a right to request access to federal government records. It is intended to complement, rather than replace, other existing processes for obtaining government information. The Access to Information Act provides the public with a legal right to access records subject to the principle that the disclosure cannot be refused and records must be produced unless they fall within the specific and limited exemptions under the Act. The Access to Information Act does not apply to your personal information. The Privacy Act gives you a limited right to:

request access to and correction of your personal information held by the federal government, subject to specific and limited exemptions under the Act;

obtain information about the government’s policies and practices relating to the administration of the Act; and

bring a complaint to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner against a federal institution for refusing to grant you access to your personal information, for improperly refusing to correct your personal information or for not following the government’s policies and practices relating to the administration of the Act. The Privacy Act does not give you a right to obtain records about another individual’s personal information. If you are not sure which act should be used for your particular case, you may wish to consult the infographic. What is personal information? Personal information means information about an identifiable individual that is recorded in any form. See section 3 of the Privacy Act. Examples of personal information include:

name;

age;

address;

education and job history;

medical and criminal history;

financial history; and

personal opinions or views, except on a third party. Personal information does not include business information such as a person’s work or business telephone number, address, or other details about where they work. Personal opinions or views about a third party that are unrelated to the public functions or public duties of an individual are not considered personal information about the individual who holds those opinions or views. For example, just because you have a personnel file in your office, or your office has personal opinions and views about you, does not make that information about you personal information. It would only be personal information if your office has your opinions or views about a third party, and if those opinions or views reveal any information about you, such as a conflict of interest. What is a government institution? A government institution means “any department or ministry of state of the Government of Canada, or any body or office, listed in the schedule; and, any parent Crown corporation, and any wholly-owned subsidiary of such a corporation, within the meaning of section 83 of the Financial Administration Act.” See section 3 of the Access to Information Act. What is a record? A record includes “any correspondence, memorandum, book, plan, map, drawing, diagram, pictorial or graphic work, photograph, film, microform, sound recording, videotape, machine readable record, and any other documentary material, regardless of physical form or characteristics, and any copy of any of those things.” See section 3 of the Access to Information Act. What is a third party? A third party is a person, institution or business other than you, the requestor, or the government institution that has control of the records being requested. See section 23 of the Access to Information Act. What is a Cabinet confidence? A Cabinet confidence means “… any record that contains any type of information that would reveal a decision, intention or recommendation that has been made or considered by the Queen’s Privy Council for Canada (Cabinet), or by a minister, or by a department and minister acting together, in relation to the making of any government policy, the development of any government policy, the formulation of any recommendation to Cabinet or to the Queen’s Privy Council for Canada (Cabinet) by a minister of the Crown, or the making of any decision or recommendation by a minister of the Crown as to the outcome of any matter relating to the formulation or implementation of any government policy.” See section 69 of the Access to Information Act. What are excluded records? Excluded records means “records respecting any of the following, namely, national defence, external affairs, including defence research, development and production, and direct activities of the Government of Canada in support of those programs, and the detection, prevention or suppression of subversive or hostile activities, including espionage and sabotage, whether on or outside Canada; activities of the Canadian Forces;

